---
title: "Install on macOS (Apple Silicon)"
description: Run KoboldCpp on an Apple Silicon Mac, allow it in Privacy & Security, and what to do on an Intel Mac.
sidebar:
  label: "macOS"
  order: 4
---

KoboldCpp has a ready-made file for Macs with Apple Silicon (M-series) and macOS 14 (Sonoma) or newer. It is a single file that you run from Terminal.

{% steps %}

1. Download [`koboldcpp-mac-arm64`](https://github.com/LostRuins/koboldcpp/releases/latest/download/koboldcpp-mac-arm64).

2. In Terminal, go to the folder with the file, make it executable and run it:

   ```sh {% title="Terminal" frame="terminal" %}
   cd ~/Downloads
   chmod +x koboldcpp-mac-arm64
   ./koboldcpp-mac-arm64
   ```

3. The first time, macOS shows "Apple could not verify “koboldcpp-mac-arm64” is free of malware…". Click **Done**, not **Move to Trash**. Open **System Settings → Privacy & Security**, scroll down to **Security** and click **Open Anyway** next to the message about `koboldcpp-mac-arm64`. Run the file again and confirm. After that, it starts without asking.

{% /steps %}

To skip the prompt, remove the download mark in Terminal before the first start:

```sh {% title="Terminal" frame="terminal" %}
xattr -d com.apple.quarantine koboldcpp-mac-arm64
```

The launcher opens. Continue with [Choose a model](/getting-started/model/).

[Apple's guide](https://support.apple.com/en-us/102445) and a [video guide](https://youtube.com/watch?v=NOW5dyA_JgY) show how to allow the file.

<!-- SCREENSHOT: Privacy & Security with the prompt to allow koboldcpp-mac-arm64 -->

## GPU (Metal)

The Mac build uses Metal on the GPU automatically.

- With **GPU Layers** at -1 (the default), all layers go to the GPU. The console prints:

  ```text
  MacOS detected: Auto GPU layers set to maximum
  ```
- `Unable to determine GPU Memory` and `Unable to determine available RAM` in the console are expected on Apple Silicon and not a problem. When the launcher opens, it can also print `Unable to detect VRAM.`.

More: [Apple Silicon](/docs/hardware/apple-silicon/).

## Advanced: failsafe mode (no Metal)

`--failsafe` switches to a CPU-only library that uses Apple's Accelerate framework instead of Metal:

```sh {% title="Terminal" frame="terminal" %}
./koboldcpp-mac-arm64 --failsafe
```

## Intel Macs

There is no ready-made file for Intel (x86) Macs. Clone the repository and compile it yourself; see [Build from source](/docs/install/from-source/#macos). Intel Macs are poorly supported.

